    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "The reason to import this one is the footprint, not the theorem. Three things become visible that no other fact in this ledger shows.\n\n(1) THE TWO KERNELS DISAGREE ON HOW TO SPELL A FOOTPRINT. Lean's `#print axioms` reports three names; `Kernel::axiom_footprint` reports six, adding `Quot`, `Quot.mk` and `Quot.lift` because our kernel classifies the entire quotient package as `Declaration::Quotient`. Both are correct in their own vocabulary, ours is the more conservative reading, and the disagreement is recorded rather than reconciled -- the same theorem checked by two kernels has two correct footprints of different length. Reconciling them silently would be the precise error `proof_route` exists to prevent.\n\n(2) IT IS NOT `F:excluded-middle`. That fact is `(assert (or p (not p)))` over a BOOLEAN `p`, settled on `smt-term-level` by exhaustive evaluation of 2 assignments. This one quantifies over all of `Prop`, which no exhaustive evaluation can reach, and it is not intuitionistically provable -- hence the three Lean axioms. Neither fact supersedes the other and neither footprint is comparable to the other.\n\n(3) IT IS NOT REACHABLE ON OUR `kernel-lean` ROUTE AT ALL. This project's logic prelude is intuitionistic and measured axiom-free, so excluded middle over `Prop` is unobtainable there without adding an axiom (ADR-0453). Importing it does not change that: what we now hold is a checked record of Lean's proof and its cost in axioms, not a constructed proof of our own.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
